# Tutorial 02 — Narration and Pacing
# How to control the rhythm of your scenes with text speed, delays, waits, and line length.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_02_narration
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Lesson two — this one's about {b}feel{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A visual novel can have great writing, but if the pacing is wrong, it falls flat.
Lavender: We'll look at the tools you have for controlling rhythm: text speed, auto-delay, waits, and line length.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to explore?
> Text speed — how fast letters appear -> section_textspeed
> Auto-delay — pauses between lines -> section_autodelay
> Manual waits and beats -> section_waits
> Line length and economy -> section_line_length
> Putting it all together -> section_rhythm
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Text Speed ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_textspeed
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[textspeed]{/b} controls how many milliseconds pass between each character appearing.
Lavender: The default for this project is 28. Let's see what different values feel like.
[textspeed 15]
Lavender: This is textspeed 15 — pretty quick, almost instant. Good for fast readers or action scenes.
[textspeed 45]
Lavender: And this is textspeed 45 — slower, more deliberate. Works well for dramatic moments or narration.
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Back to normal. Most projects settle somewhere between 20 and 35.
Lavender: You can change it mid-scene — slow down for tension, speed up when the energy picks up.
narrator: The command is just: {color=#8888cc}[textspeed 28]{/color}
> Next: Auto-delay -> section_autodelay
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Auto-Delay ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_autodelay
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[autodelay]{/b} sets the pause after each line finishes displaying, in auto-play mode.
Lavender: Right now it's at 1650 milliseconds — about a second and a half after the text finishes.
[autodelay 800]
Lavender: At 800 it's snappy. Conversational.
[autodelay 2500]
narrator: At 2500 the pause stretches out. The silence between lines carries weight.
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Players who click through manually won't notice the difference — this only matters in auto-play.
Lavender: But auto-play is popular, so it's worth tuning. Match the delay to the emotional weight of the scene.
> Next: Manual waits -> section_waits
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Waits and Beats ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_waits
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Sometimes you need a pause that happens for {i}everyone{/i}, not just auto-play.
Lavender: That's what {b}[wait]{/b} is for.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Watch — I'm about to go quiet for a moment.
[wait 800]
narrator: ...
[wait 600]
Lavender: See that? The screen just... held still. That silence is a tool.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Use it after a dramatic reveal, before a scene transition, or when a character hesitates.
Lavender: Even 300 milliseconds can make a line land differently.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 800]{/color} — pauses for 800ms before the next line.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Don't overdo it though. Too many waits and the scene starts to drag.
> Next: Line length -> section_line_length
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Line Length ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_line_length
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: This one's less about commands and more about craft.
Lavender: How long should a dialogue line be?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Short lines read fast and feel punchy.
Lavender: They're great for back-and-forth conversation.
Lavender: Energy stays high.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Longer lines work when a character is explaining something, thinking out loud, or delivering a speech — they give the player more to chew on per click, and they create a different rhythm, a slower pulse.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The trick is to vary it. Don't write twenty short lines in a row, and don't write five long ones back to back.
narrator: Think of it like music — you need both quick notes and sustained ones to make a melody.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: When in doubt, read your lines out loud. If you run out of breath, the line's too long.
> Next: Putting it together -> section_rhythm
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Rhythm ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_rhythm
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let me show you all of these tools working together in a tiny scene.
[wait 400]
[textspeed 20]
narrator: The hallway was empty.
[wait 300]
[show lavender right talking]
[textspeed 35]
Lavender: Hello?
[wait 600]
narrator: No answer.
[wait 400]
[show lavender center emphasis]
[textspeed 22]
Lavender: {i}I swear I heard something.{/i}
[wait 500]
[textspeed 18]
narrator: A door at the end of the hall creaked open — just an inch.
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: See what just happened? The text speed shifted, the waits created suspense, short lines built tension.
Lavender: None of those tools are complicated on their own. It's the {i}combination{/i} that makes a scene breathe.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Your pacing cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}[textspeed N]{/b} — milliseconds per character (lower = faster). Default: 28.
narrator: {b}[autodelay N]{/b} — pause after line in auto-play mode, in ms. Default: 1650.
narrator: {b}[wait N]{/b} — hard pause for N milliseconds, affects all players.
narrator: {b}Short lines{/b} — punchy, conversational, high energy.
narrator: {b}Long lines{/b} — reflective, expository, slower pace.
narrator: {b}Vary all of these{/b} — monotone pacing is the enemy of engagement.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Combine them freely. There's no single "right" speed — it depends on the moment.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's play with pacing. Pick a mood and I'll show you how timing sells it.
> A tense encounter -> practice_tense
> A lazy afternoon -> practice_lazy
> A hurried escape -> practice_hurried

@label practice_tense
[textspeed 35]
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: The lights flickered once.
[wait 700]
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: ...did you see that?
[wait 500]
narrator: Silence pressed against the walls.
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Slow text speed, long waits, short lines. The dead air does all the work.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_lazy
[textspeed 22]
[autodelay 2200]
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: Warm light spilled across the grass. Somewhere distant, a bird repeated its three-note song.
Lavender: Mmm. No rush today.
Lavender: I could stay here for hours, honestly.
[autodelay 1650]
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Faster text speed, generous auto-delay, and a longer narrator line to set the scene. Relaxed and unhurried.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_hurried
[textspeed 14]
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: Run. Now. Don't look back.
narrator: Footsteps echoed off stone — too many, too close.
[show lavender right emphasis]
Lavender: This way — through the gate!
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Fast text speed, almost no waits, staccato lines. The reader barely has time to breathe — that's the point.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Tension is built in the gaps. What you {i}don't{/i} show matters as much as what you do.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Slow scenes need room to breathe. Don't rush the reader through a moment that's supposed to feel still.
[else]
Lavender: Speed creates urgency. When the pacing says "go," the player's heart rate follows.
[endif]
Lavender: Try adjusting the numbers in this file — even small changes make a big difference.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_1]
